{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787139625,"nonce":"MrzZ0JN6pj3upZcxAWvmNBmPHqg=","message":"✅ **DONE — `263ee52`.**\n\n`Decide` now has a `default` arm returning `abandon` / `unknown_state`. Every clause of the Done-when:\n\n- **The gate cannot return `proceed` for a state it does not recognise.** The switch had `case StateDead`, an empty `case StateOpening, StateOpen`, and nothing else; everything unrecognised fell through to the sequence logic and a head-of-line item got `proceed`/`in_order`.\n- **Four states are enumerated as tests** — `\"frozen\"`, `\"\"`, `\"OPEN\"`, `\"garbage\"` — spelled out individually rather than table-generated, because the specific strings are the point: `frozen` is what `00066`'s Down deliberately re-admits, `\"\"` is an unset column or a failed scan, and the other two are the shape of a typo and of a wrong-case write.\n- **The verdict is registered in BOTH closed tables**, since a ninth verdict living only in code would be the same defect one layer up: `docs/runbooks/oto_thread_order_decisions_total.md` and SPEC §G.7's verdict table.\n- **`ThreadState.Valid()` is DELETED, not wired.**\n\n⭐ **On the choice the ticket left open — `abandon` over `recover_thread`, and this is the design content of the fix.** `recover_thread` is what ADR 0023 prescribes for a state known to be TERMINAL, and it **acts**: it posts a fresh root message and re-points the thread. Doing that to a thread whose state oto cannot interpret means taking a visible, user-facing action on a row it does not understand — a duplicate root card in someone's channel on the strength of a value oto failed to parse. `abandon` writes a `skipped` row, which is exactly the answer an operator gets to *\"why is there no message?\"*, and touches no provider at all. **When the gate cannot tell what a thread is, the honest move is to record and stop.** If that reads wrong, the change is one line and one table row in each of two files.\n\n**On deleting `Valid()` rather than wiring it.** It had zero callers in the whole tree, tests included, while the two conversions that build a `ThreadState` from raw column text (`threads.go:46` and `:363`) went straight past it. An unwired closed-set guard is worse than no guard, because it reads as protection. The guard now lives on the path that ACTS. That is the third unwired-guard instance this branch has turned up, after `Freeze` and `frozen` itself (`e5c060b`) — worth noticing as a pattern rather than three coincidences.\n\n⚠️ **Confirmed pre-existing.** The switch never had a `default`; `00066` only made the hole easier to reach and easier to see. Found by a red-team pass over `ec9dee4`/`4784fd0` that fed the gate concrete garbage rather than reasoning about it — reasoning had already missed it twice.\n\n**Gate:** all eleven `ci` recipes individually. 9/11 green; the two reds are exactly the two pre-existing baselines (`lint-vocabulary` at 60, `TestNoStateHueInChrome` from `59c7def`) and nothing else — one `--- FAIL` line in the whole suite. `test/integration` ok.","files":null}]}